<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Paramara dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 1305 CE (approx.) · capital Dhar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Malwa dynasty of the scholar-king Bhoja, whose capital Dhar became a byword for learning and the patronage of poets.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Paramaras emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> in the 9th century, probably as feudatories of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gurjara-pratihara">Gurjara-Pratihara</a>. Later legend said their ancestor sprang from a sacrificial fire on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-abu">Mount Abu</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siyaka">Siyaka</a>, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a>, made the dynasty independent and sacked the Rashtrakuta capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a> in 972.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vakpati-munja">Vakpati Munja</a> was a poet and patron who fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> and was captured and executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tailapa-ii">Tailapa II</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhoja-paramara">Bhoja</a>, ruling from about 1010 to 1055, was celebrated as a polymath credited with works on poetics, architecture and medicine; he made Dhar a centre of learning, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhojeshwar-temple">Bhojpur Shiva temple</a> and was remembered in countless legends. The Paramaras also built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayeshwar-temple">Udayeshvara temple</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udaipur">Udaipur</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhoja-paramara">Bhoja</a>'s defeat by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chaulukya-dynasty">Chaulukya dynasty</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kalachuris-of-tripuri">Kalachuris of Tripuri</a>, the kingdom weakened. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> attacked repeatedly, and in 1305 an army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>, ending Paramara rule.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/paramara-dynasty.jpg" alt="Paramara dynasty" width="320" height="296"><figcaption>Image: Map created from DEMIS Mapserver, which are public domain. Koba-chan. Reference: [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Paramaras.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 1305 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Dhar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Siyaka, Vakpati Munja, Bhoja</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Shaivism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Paramaras of Malwa, Kingdom of Malwa (Paramara)</dd></dl></aside></div>

